# Gates that must have produced a report before the tree can be called good.
#
# This file is the half of the mechanism that an exit code cannot supply.
# A gate that never runs leaves no report; without a list of gates that
# ought to exist, "no report" and "nothing to report" are the same thing.
# A CI gate in this repository sat behind an already-red target for its
# whole life and was counted as passing the entire time.
#
# One gate name per line.  A trailing `?' marks the gate optional: it is
# shown when present, and its absence is not a failure.
#
# Add a line here the moment a gate becomes load-bearing.  Removing a
# line is a decision to stop caring about that gate — make it explicitly,
# not by letting the gate quietly stop emitting.

compile
ert-full

# Tree-wide checks that already ratchet against a baseline.  Each now
# prints a GATE-COUNT line so "clean" and "scanned nothing" can be told
# apart; run them through `nelisp-ai.sh gate NAME -- make TARGET'.
parens-check

# The standalone build emits Elisp programs as string literals, whose parens
# `parens-check' cannot see: it reads the .el file, not the text it produces.
# One dropped paren nested the artifact command dispatch inside an `unless'
# that never runs, and `compile-elisp-artifact' silently did nothing for two
# days before a bisect found it.  This one parses the generated text.
generated-source-parse

unsafe-inventory
ns-inventory
reader-surface-audit
pkg-graph
pkg-load-lists
fallback-inventory
bootstrap-contract

# Developer-loop gates: useful, but not evidence about the whole tree.
ert-focus?
ns?

# Namespace boundaries for the recipe skeletons.  Whole-tree namespace
# checking is `make ns-inventory', which ratchets against a baseline;
# this one requires the skeletons to be simply clean, since they are
# what people copy.
ns-recipes?

# Recipe smokes: run them when touching recipes/, or to find out whether
# an application shape still works on this machine.
recipe-stdio-service?
recipe-batch-data?
recipe-native-hotpath?
recipe-native-hotpath-exec?
recipe-checked-resources?

# Measurements.  A bench gate passes when the ratio is valid, skips with
# a reason when the machine invalidated it, and fails only when the two
# arms are the same program.  It never fails on the number itself.
bench-borrow-check?

# The price of adopting nl-safe in the stdio service, measured the same
# way.  See recipes/stdio-service/README.org.
bench-service-borrow?

# What the binary in front of you can actually do.  Optional: it needs a
# binary, and on a machine without one it reports a reasoned skip.
runtime-probe?

# Elisp to wasm32, validated and executed under node.  Optional only
# because it needs node; it passes on both hosts measured so far.
wasm-smoke?

# Standalone gates.  These build a binary from the tree and run it, so
# they need a Linux x86_64 host.  Everywhere else the default target
# (linux-x86_64) is not runnable, `nelisp-standalone--target-runnable-on-host-p'
# is nil, and the gate prints GATE-SKIP with a reason rather than exiting 0
# -- which used to read as a pass.  `verify' accepts a reasoned skip for a
# required gate, so requiring this one does not paint macOS or Windows red.
#
# standalone-reader-test was OPTIONAL because it was red on Linux, and had
# been before this contract reached it: `require' began signalling
# `file-missing' instead of silently succeeding (a good change), and the
# standalone path carried requires that only ever worked because it did not.
# Measured 2026-08-18 in WSL Debian, failing at the fifth of its eight
# checks with `file-missing: url-parse'.
#
# cd64c0bd7 (2026-08-19) resolved it, by measuring what the tree actually
# needs rather than assuming: every name the code uses from `subr-x' is
# already defined by the stdlib prelude, and `url-parse'/`url-util' are
# wanted only so the primitive install loop finds real function objects on
# a real host -- a loop that already guards each entry with `fboundp'.  The
# requires were made optional; the functions were never missing, only the
# files.
#
# Promoted 2026-08-21, on measurement rather than on that reasoning:
#
#   HEAD  8 of 8 checks, 0 findings -- twice, once from a wiped target/
#   main  fails at 5 of 8, `file-missing: url-parse' -- the 08-18 shape
#
# main is an ancestor of HEAD, so the fix and this line merge together and
# there is no window where the requirement outruns the fix.
#
# It is not in `nelisp-ai.sh check', for the reason `ert-full' is not: it
# builds a binary.  `nelisp-ai.sh standalone' produces the report, and
# `verify' prints its age.
standalone-reader-test

# nelisp-native-artifact-gate is the second half of the same unblocking, and
# guards the contract Nelix needs before it can rely on precompiled elisp:
# bulk .neln builds, `--native-policy required' proving every top-level defun
# entered native code, the audit reporting coverage gaps, and the
# load/eval/exec commands preferring the adjacent artifact.  Two of its nine
# cases are negatives -- a required policy that must refuse an unsupported
# target, an audit that must reject a missing artifact -- so it fails if the
# contract stops being enforced, not only if it stops working.
#
# Wired and promoted 2026-08-21, measured the same way:
#
#   HEAD                9 of 9 cases -- twice, once from a wiped target/
#   main's binary       fails at case 3, `file-missing: url-parse'
#
# That control is the whole argument.  It ran HEAD's script against a binary
# built from main, so the only variable is the binary: same host, same script,
# same minute.  It also showed the partial count working -- GATE-COUNT
# checked=2 findings=1 -- so a failure says how far it got rather than
# disappearing into "no GATE-COUNT line".
#
# Skips the same way and for the same reason as standalone-reader-test, by
# asking `--target-runnable-on-host-p' rather than keeping its own uname
# table.  A predicate that cannot be asked is an error, not a skip: those two
# are told apart by exit code, since folding them together is how a broken
# invocation starts reading as a reasoned skip.
#
# `nelisp-ai.sh native-artifact' produces the report.  Out of `check'
# alongside standalone-reader-test, for the reason ert-full is: it builds a
# binary.
nelisp-native-artifact-gate
#
# nelisp-performance-gate and standalone-selfhost-test were the last two of the
# four that shared the `file-missing: url-parse' defect.  Both were past it and
# failing on their own, and both are wired and required as of 2026-08-21.
#
# nelisp-performance-gate exercises the pair the artifact runtime cache exists
# for: the same module loaded and evaluated through the cache and through the
# source fallback, plus the build restoring the enable marker it removes.  Only
# the cache half had ever run green.  The source half failed at `fallback_load'
# with `wrong-type-argument sequencep nil', which was the reader turning
# `:preloads nil' into a symbol NAMED "nil" -- one that prints as nil and
# answers nil to `null', `listp' and `sequencep' alike.  Fixed in
# `nelisp-read--sexp'.
#
# standalone-selfhost-test is the standalone interpreter loading its own
# compiler toolchain as source and emitting a native x86_64 ELF with no Emacs
# in the loop.  It failed at `file-missing: nelisp-asm-arm64' because the
# driver concatenated its files in the wrong dependency order -- harmless while
# `require' silently succeeded on a missing file, fatal once it signalled.
# Behind that were two more: `nelisp-aot-compiler.el' pulled the whole CC
# pipeline in through one top-level require, and the assembler counted and
# indexed its own output in CHARACTERS, so a two-byte instruction whose bytes
# form valid UTF-8 read back as one character 455 and failed a byte range
# check.
#
# Both skip the same way as the other standalone gates, by asking
# `--target-runnable-on-host-p'.  `nelisp-ai.sh perf' and `nelisp-ai.sh
# selfhost' produce the reports; both build a binary, so both stay out of
# `check'.
nelisp-performance-gate
standalone-selfhost-test

# ===================================================================
# The 2026-08-21 sweep: every gate-like make target, measured
# ===================================================================
#
# 49 gate-like targets in the Makefile were not listed here, and nothing was
# running them.  All 49 were measured.  What follows is where each one landed,
# so the next person does not have to run the sweep again to find out.
#
# Five defects came out of it, four of them the same defect -- `length' and
# `aref' counting CHARACTERS where bytes were meant, in the arm64 assembler and
# in five sites of the ELF writer.  A 120-byte .text section measured 118.
# Details in the commit; the point here is that these gates existed and were
# not run, so the defects sat.
#
# GREEN AND WIRED ABOVE: the reader smokes are covered by
# `standalone-reader-test' (12 checks), which now includes the arm64
# byte-identity check and the `intern'/`intern-soft' "nil"/"t"
# canonicalization check.  Beside it stand 28 individual `standalone-reader-*'
# targets, each measured green on 2026-08-21 -- two of them only after the
# fixes in this sweep.  Named in full so a later reader can tell "measured and
# green" from "nobody looked":
#
#   standalone-reader-catch-throw-tag-smoke   standalone-reader-checked
#   standalone-reader-checked-soak            standalone-reader-cond-let-shape-smoke
#   standalone-reader-current-time-smoke      standalone-reader-declare-strip-smoke
#   standalone-reader-derived-mode-shape-smoke standalone-reader-elt-smoke
#   standalone-reader-ffi-smoke  (was red: print-circle in the unit cache key)
#   standalone-reader-fmt-smoke               standalone-reader-getenv-smoke
#   standalone-reader-intern-soft-loop-smoke  standalone-reader-intern-soft-smoke
#   standalone-reader-load-smoke              standalone-reader-match-data-smoke
#   standalone-reader-mod-float-smoke  (was red: `mod' with a negative divisor)
#   standalone-reader-nested-backquote-macro-smoke
#   standalone-reader-number-token-smoke      standalone-reader-pcase-quote-literal-smoke
#   standalone-reader-prelude-equal-reload-smoke  standalone-reader-prelude-test
#   standalone-reader-process-smoke           standalone-reader-realrt-smoke
#   standalone-reader-recursion-guard-smoke   standalone-reader-repl-smoke
#   standalone-reader-require-provide-smoke   standalone-reader-shadow-smoke
#   standalone-reader-tls-smoke
#
# GREEN AND NOW WIRED: these were measured clean by the sweep and had no
# report, so `verify' could not hold anyone to them.  Each now prints a
# GATE-COUNT line and is required below.  Nine needed the line added; four
# already printed one and only needed listing.
#
# Split by cost, because that decides where they run:
#
#   in `check' (seconds, no binary):
#     prelude-toplevel-check  partial-inventory  gate-selfcheck  ns-gate
#     nl-check-gate  fallback-inventory-selftest  wasm-dtw-skeleton-smoke
#
#   `nelisp-ai.sh extras' (each builds a binary):
#     nelisp-runtime-image-cache-gate  nelisp-source-command-substrate-gate
#     standalone-chunk-growth-test  standalone-parallel-compile-test
#     standalone-selfhost-mt-test  wasm-runtime-image-smoke
#
#   `nelisp-ai.sh smokes':
#     standalone-reader-smokes -- the 28 individual reader smokes as ONE gate.
#     28 separate reports would demand 28 freshness checks for one build's
#     worth of evidence; the count is the signal, and a target that stops
#     existing shows up as a smaller number.
#
prelude-toplevel-check
partial-inventory
gate-selfcheck
ns-gate
nl-check-gate
fallback-inventory-selftest
wasm-dtw-skeleton-smoke
nelisp-runtime-image-cache-gate
nelisp-source-command-substrate-gate
standalone-chunk-growth-test
standalone-parallel-compile-test
standalone-selfhost-mt-test
standalone-eval-test
wasm-runtime-image-smoke
standalone-reader-smokes
# nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate: promoted 2026-08-21, see "THE nelix GATES,
# PART 2" below -- also `nelisp-ai.sh extras' (needs target/nelisp built,
# does not build it).
nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate
#
# CANNOT RUN HERE, AND NOW SAY SO: three of these exit 0 with a GATE-SKIP line
# and a reason, which `verify' accepts for a required gate.  They are not
# listed as required yet only because nothing has run their success path --
# adding a wrapper whose green has never been seen is the unverified-gate
# problem in a new place -- but the skip is no longer indistinguishable from a
# failure:
#
#   macho-acceptance-test   GATE-SKIP macho acceptance requires macOS.  Also
#                           counts its 8 checks now, so a macOS runner gets a
#                           number rather than silence.
#   wasm-dtw-smoke          GATE-SKIP newDTW-nelisp checkout absent.  The
#   wasm-dtw-site-smoke     transpile step decides it, so the whole chain skips
#                           together; DTW_GAME_ROOT points it at a checkout.
#                           (Its compile step also asked for `--kind neln' with
#                           a `.wasm' output, the same mismatch fixed in
#                           wasm-runtime-image-smoke -- corrected here too, so
#                           a machine that HAS the checkout gets past it.)
#
# THE nelix GATES, PART 2 (2026-08-21): both recorded defects below were run
# down to file:line and one of the two is fixed.  `../nelix' is a separate
# repository (its own git history, last commit 2026-06-25 before this pass)
# reached by three make targets; they were filed above as "needs an external
# checkout" and that was already known to be wrong -- the checkout exists and
# the gates reach it.  This pass re-measured each of the three from scratch
# (`make nelisp-nelix-command-gate`, `-operational-gate`, `-native-hot-gate`
# from this repo's root) before touching anything, to have an against-the-bug
# baseline rather than trusting the prose above.
#
#   nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate -- FIXED, now green, promoted below.
#     Baseline matched the record exactly: reaches the nelix checkout, passes
#     four checks, then `expect_out standalone_subset_audit_id_native' fails
#     with expected="ok\tfalse\n...bat\nbackend\tnix\n" (literal backslash-t /
#     backslash-n) vs. actual containing real TAB/NEWLINE bytes.  Byte-level
#     capture (`xxd` on the artifact's raw stdout) showed the actual value is
#     `"ok<TAB>false<NL>present<TAB>ripgrep<NL>...nix<NL>"' -- a real
#     Elisp `prin1-to-string' rendering of that string, wrapped in quotes,
#     control characters left untouched.  Verified against stock host Emacs
#     30.1: `(prin1-to-string "a\tb\nc")` also embeds a real TAB/NEWLINE, not
#     the two-character sequences -- `print-escape-newlines' is nil by
#     default and tab is never escaped regardless.  So nelisp's native output
#     was already correct and the defect was the gate's own hard-coded
#     expectation, in THIS repo, not nelix: `tools/nelisp-nelix-native-hot-
#     gate.sh:180` and `:189` (`standalone_subset_audit_id_native' and
#     `standalone_subset_upgrade_id_native') now build the expected value
#     with `printf' so `\t'/`\n' are read as real bytes, matching every other
#     payload literal already in this script.  Re-measured after the fix:
#     6 of 6 checks, `nelix_native_hot_gate_result label=nelix_native_hot_gate
#     rc=0` -- twice, once through `nelisp-ai.sh gate ... -- make
#     nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate`.  Also gained a `GATE-COUNT' line (it had
#     none before; `nelisp-ai.sh gate' was reading a green run as "no
#     GATE-COUNT line", i.e. a failure by omission) -- see the sweep note
#     below.
#
#   nelisp-nelix-command-gate / nelisp-nelix-operational-gate -- ONE defect
#     fixed, a SECOND, DIFFERENT one is now the block; still red.  Baseline
#     matched the record: `file-missing: "nelix-compat.el"' from the first
#     direct-runtime call (`nelisp_direct_list').  Root cause was in
#     `dev/nelix', confirmed by tracing the actual signal (`file-missing',
#     not `require's own `file-error' shape) to
#     `dev/nelix/scripts/nelix-nelisp-smoke.el's `nelix-nelisp-smoke--load-
#     compat', which called `(load "nelix-compat.el")' -- a bare relative
#     filename.  NeLisp's standalone `load' primitive
#     (`nelisp--builtin-load' in this repo's `src/nelisp-load.el') treats its
#     argument as a literal path and never searches a load-path (only
#     `require'/`nelisp-locate-file' do); every OTHER cross-file reference in
#     `bin/nelix' already passes an absolute path in a dedicated variable
#     (`nelix-nelisp-smoke-*-source'), so this one bare string was the actual
#     gap, not a load-path that was never set.  Setting NELIX_LISPDIR alone
#     never reached this call at all, which is why the earlier note that it
#     "does not help" was correct as stated.  Fixed in `dev/nelix': a new
#     `nelix-nelisp-smoke-compat-source' variable
#     (`scripts/nelix-nelisp-smoke.el:58`, consumed at `:184`) that
#     `bin/nelix:2225` resolves to `${NELIX_LISPDIR:-<repo>}/nelix-compat.el'
#     (so NELIX_LISPDIR now actually reaches this path, the documented knob
#     made functional) and `bin/nelix:2259` sets before the direct runner
#     calls `nelix-nelisp-smoke-preload-suite-runtime'.  Re-measured: the
#     `file-missing' error is gone.
#
#     Past that point a SECOND, unrelated defect is reached and is still
#     open: `void-function: (defvaralias)`, from `nelix-core.el:95`'s
#     `(defvaralias 'nelix-default-backend 'nelix-core-default-backend)' (and
#     four more calls just below it) -- ordinary top-level Elisp that host
#     Emacs's `--runtime emacs' path runs fine, but NeLisp's standalone
#     runtime has never implemented `defvaralias'.  This is NOT the same
#     class of bug as the first one and is NOT a quick addition: the
#     standalone `target/nelisp' reader-path binary this gate actually runs
#     does not read `src/nelisp-eval.el's generic `nelisp--install-
#     primitives' as ordinary Elisp at build time -- confirmed by adding a
#     `defvaralias' primitive there exactly the way `defalias' already sits
#     (same function, same registration call) and rebuilding
#     (`make standalone-reader'): `defalias'/`boundp'/`require' remain
#     `fboundp' in the rebuilt binary, `defvaralias' does not.  The binary is
#     instead linked from a hand-curated table of native compiled units
#     (`scripts/nelisp-standalone-build.el:12509`,
#     `nelisp-standalone--manifest', plus a second `...--reader-extra-
#     manifest' of hand-written machine-code fragments with explicit
#     register-ABI and GC-arena-epoch bookkeeping) -- adding a primitive
#     there means writing a new compiled unit through that pipeline, not a
#     one-line Elisp addition, and a wrong arena-epoch annotation on
#     anything that writes a new global binding is the silent-corruption
#     class of bug this repo's own `nelisp-cc' commit history warns about.
#     That is deep enough to guess wrong, so the speculative `nelisp-eval.el'
#     addition was reverted rather than kept half-working; `target/nelisp'
#     was rebuilt back to a clean HEAD binary and reconfirmed at the same
#     `defvaralias' failure before recording this.  Nothing here should be
#     "papered over" by rewriting `nelix-core.el' to avoid `defvaralias'
#     either: that call is ordinary, idiomatic Elisp that already works
#     under host Emacs, so the gap is NeLisp's language coverage, not
#     nelix's code -- the opposite direction from the first defect, and the
#     same principle (fix belongs on the side that is actually wrong).
#     `nelisp-nelix-command-gate' and `nelisp-nelix-operational-gate' stay
#     unpromoted and unwired below until that gap has an owner.
#
# Both scripts are deliberately NOT given a GATE-SKIP for either defect:
# skipping a gate that runs and finds a real defect is exactly how a check
# starts passing by not running.
#
# `nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate' is required below on the strength of the
# green measurement above.  It needs `target/nelisp' already built, the same
# precondition every `nelisp-ai.sh extras' member already has (none of the
# six build the standalone binary themselves either -- they consume it, same
# as this one), so it joins that tier rather than `check'.  It joined without
# a `GATE-COUNT' line before this pass; it has one now (6 checks, one per
# `run_timed' call, via an EXIT trap so a failing run still reports how far
# it got instead of vanishing into "no GATE-COUNT line").  `nelisp-nelix-
# command-gate' got the identical trap+counter treatment (22 checks) so a
# fixed run reports honestly too, but it is not listed as required and not
# wired into any tier below -- it is still red on the `defvaralias' defect
# above, and requiring a gate whose green side nobody has seen is the same
# problem this file exists to prevent.  `nelisp-nelix-operational-gate' has
# no script of its own (a Makefile dependency on the other two) and follows
# whichever of them is weaker; it stays unrequired for the same reason.
#
# gate-mutation injects a real defect in front of a gate and asks whether the
# gate goes red.  On 2026-08-21 two of five did not, and it was listed here as
# correctly-red rather than required -- a true report, not a failure.  Both are
# fixed, so it is 5/5 and required:
#
#   ns-gate stayed green when `round' diverged from its lisp/ mirror, because
#   the accepted-set key was kind+subject+files.  Once a divergence is
#   accepted, the two definitions could drift into any other shape and still
#   match.  The key now carries a digest of the definitions, so accepting a
#   divergence accepts THAT divergence rather than the name.  (Re-pinned: the
#   key changed, so the accepted set was regenerated from the clean tree --
#   144 entries, same as before.)
#
#   parity-coverage stayed green when a covered name stopped being mentioned,
#   because it ratchets on a COUNT and was running 102 above its floor.  A
#   count cannot see a specific name leave.  The baseline now also pins the
#   259 names the corpus covers today; losing one fails and says which.  The
#   floor went 157 -> 259 in the same pass: a floor 102 below the measurement
#   is not a floor.
#
# Later the same day, the same lesson was generalized to the other
# baseline/ratchet gates in `tools/ai/nelisp-ai.sh check': every count-only
# ratchet over an INVENTORY OF BAD THINGS (not a curated accept-list) can
# hide a swap the same way -- one entry resolves, a different one appears,
# the total does not move.  Four gates got the ns-gate/parity-coverage
# treatment (an identity-pinned baseline alongside the count, regenerated
# from the clean tree), and 5/5 became 10/10:
#
#   unsafe-inventory pins `pinned-kernel-touch' (87 kernel files that touch
#   raw memory, replacing the bare `kernel-files' count in
#   tools/unsafe-kernel.txt) and `pinned-kernel-call FILE COUNT' (the 3
#   kernel files with an unquoted `unsafe-call', replacing the bare `total'
#   in tools/unsafe-inventory-baseline.txt).  Verified against the exact
#   defect: moving one call from `nelisp-flat-arena-probe.el' to
#   `nelisp-cc-alloc-dealloc.el' left `kernel-files' at 87 and `total' at
#   538, unmoved, and the new pins caught it by name.
#
#   ns-inventory pins `pinned-collision KEY' for `ns-collision' and
#   `ns-collision-divergent' (279 entries), KEY in `nl-ns-finding-key' shape
#   -- the same kind+subject+files+shape key ns-gate's fix introduced, reused
#   here rather than reinvented.  `ns-collision' also dropped 178 -> 157,
#   its measured value; the ceiling had drifted the same way parity-coverage's
#   had.  `ns-prefix-violation' (853) and `ns-private-escape' (611) stay
#   count-only, and say why in tools/nl-ns-inventory.el: they are roughly
#   8x the size of the pinned pair, `ns-private-escape' is an explicitly
#   CONCEDED and growing debt rather than a curated set, and most of
#   `ns-prefix-violation' is public API surface already visible through
#   `nl-check-gate' and `ns-gate' -- a reasoned exception, not a silent one.
#
#   fallback-inventory pins `pinned-site KEY COUNT' across all four kinds
#   (113 keys, 119 sites -- COUNT because two structurally identical
#   handlers in one toplevel hash to the same key, and presence alone would
#   let a third land unpinned).  Regenerating this baseline found the tree
#   had already drifted past it unnoticed (49/37/33 measured against
#   48/32/32 checked in) -- caught up in the same commit as the pins, the
#   same live demonstration parity-coverage's own history gives for why a
#   number needs a name attached.
#
#   pkg-graph pins `pinned-host-require FEATURE FILE' (1 entry).  Verified
#   by swapping `filenotify' for an unrelated `ert-x' require in the same
#   file: `hard-host-require' stayed at 1, and the pin caught the new
#   feature by name.
#
#   generated-source-parse had no mutation case at all.  It now does: a
#   closing paren dropped from a `"(defvar features nil)\n"' string literal
#   inside `nelisp-standalone--artifact-command-cache-src', the same defect
#   class -- and the same generator -- the gate was built to catch on
#   2026-08-19.
#
# Each was verified red-then-green against the real swap before being
# folded into the row below, not only against a plain addition.
gate-mutation
#
# WAS RED, AND OPEN; NOW FIXED AND REQUIRED: standalone-eval-test failed at
# link, not at runtime -- `shim.o' called `nl_gc_ctx_pop', defined only in
# the reader-only `nelisp-standalone--gc-source', which the eval manifest did
# not list (measured before: `Error: nelisp-link--unresolved-symbol
# ("nl_gc_ctx_pop" "shim.o")').  The direction recorded here at the time
# (move reader-only code out of shared units, per `--applyfn-census-helpers')
# was the right one but incomplete: fixing it uncovered four more of the same
# shape, each only visible once the linker got past the one before it --
#
#   nl_gc_ctx_push/_pop, nl_gc_eval_ctx_push/_pop -- core eval-path plumbing
#   (`nelisp_eval_call''s only caller) that was misfiled in `gc-source';
#   moved to `nelisp-standalone--shim-source', shim.o's own unit.
#
#   nl_fa_write_all/_read_all, bf_arena_dump_image_to_file and the rest of
#   the flat-arena persistence + swizzle family in `--applyfn-core-helpers'
#   -- reader-only (OS file primitives + real GC mark/arena calls), dead in
#   the baked build (their dispatch arms sit past the baked table's 19-entry
#   cutoff); excised to `--applyfn-fa-file-helpers' and (one function,
#   sharing the census family's own reason) `--applyfn-census-helpers'.
#
#   bf_sig_copy32 -- core eval plumbing (`eval-inner.o'/`combiner-cons.o'/
#   `combiner-apply.o' all call it directly to stash a signal), misfiled in
#   the reader-only `--applyfn-bf-helpers'; moved to `--applyfn-core-helpers'.
#
#   nl_gc_free_block/_in_arena/_is_boot/_mark_slot -- `arglist.o',
#   `rootstack.o', `val-load.o' and `mirror-lookup.o' each call one of these
#   directly for their own real arena bookkeeping (not dead code, unlike the
#   flat-arena family above); `reader-gc.o' itself is self-contained (no
#   dependency on any reader-only I/O unit) and the reader build already
#   links it unconditionally, so it was added to the eval manifest too,
#   under the same name, rather than duplicating or stubbing its functions.
#
# Measured after: `make standalone-eval-test' builds (47 linked units) and
# passes (`[standalone] PASS: ... -> exit 3 (expected 3)').
#
# `make audit' is deliberately absent: it exits 1 while sub-phases are
# PENDING (26 at the 2026-08-21 measurement), which is a roadmap state
# rather than a defect, and a gate that is red by design trains people
# to ignore the column.
#
# NOT GATES, and listed so the absence is a decision rather than an oversight:
# `test', `test-fast', `test-jit', `test-nojit', `test-one' and
# `test-parallel' are ERT drivers over the same suite `ert-full' already
# reports on -- listing them would count the same evidence six more times.
# `release-checksum' and `alloc-check-collect' are tooling that produces
# artifacts, not checks that can pass or fail.
